<!-- has — an ENTITY, a NAME, and what it HOLDS. The boot seed writes the relation and this shelf
     answers for it everywhere else:

         <<~ has rank pulse "levels/0-4 reads/a-mark-a-morpheme-a-word">>

     Thirty-five carriers each declared a local shorthand for exactly this, hard-coding the entity
     into the procedure's own name — seventy-two names, one shape. The entity rides as the FIRST
     POSITIONAL here, so one definition answers for all of them.

     WHAT AN ENTITY HOLDS RIDES QUOTED. It carries slashes, colons, arrows and prose; unquoted, every
     `word:` binds a parameter nobody named and the slot receives nothing.

     AND IT RIDES AS TEXT. What an entity holds carries square brackets, arrows and backticks — a
     filtered transclusion renders each as a LINK to a tiddler nobody wrote, and wikitext would read
     `[Fiduciary: POA]` as a wikilink. A value names no address. -->

\procedure ~has(p1:"" p2:"" p3:"")
<span class="lar-has"><<p1>> <<p2>> holds [<$text text=<<p3>>/>]</span>
\end
